idence directly covers 2023. WEB-015 uses 2022 incidence figures published in 2023 and is explicitly contextual. WEB-019/020 are 2024 publications documenting November–December 2023 events. No later event was used to expand the target period silently.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Direct Scholz evidence is strongest in WEB-003 and WEB-023. Most domestic-policy, rights, and economic items are coalition, ministry, parliamentary, judicial, Länder, or country-level evidence and are labeled accordingly.\n- **Weak/context-only items:** LP-004 and LP-005 are context. WEB-001/002 are gateways only. WEB-015 is near-period context. Official claims are not treated as independent proof of success.\n- **Independent corroboration gaps:** Nuclear policy, arms-recipient controls, social-service outcomes, personal integrity beyond Cum-Ex, and post-failure adaptation remain under-corroborated.\n- **Source concentration:** NATO PA carries three separate international/security facts; Freedom House and HRW carry much of the rights record; Clean Energy Wire carries two budget-consequence facts. Those are same-URL splits with distinct locators and claims, not duplicate evidence.\n- **Cross-chapter reuse:** Protest evidence links 3B/4B; Bürgergeld links 5B/6B/8B; the budget judgment links 4B/5B/8B; Ukraine strategy links 1B/2B/8B; Cum-Ex scrutiny links 4B/7B.\n- **Client matrix:** Not used.\n- **Scores/ranking:** None assigned."}}
